"""Original kitchen decision guide, with reusable HomeCostEngine budget methods."""
from pathlib import Path
import runpy,copy
base=runpy.run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('renovation-blueprint.py')))
CHAPTERS=[]
def chapter(title,deck,paragraphs,example,rows,conclusion,prompts,checks,task):
    CHAPTERS.append(dict(title=title,deck=deck,paragraphs=paragraphs,example=example,rows=rows,conclusion=conclusion,prompts=prompts,checks=checks,task=task))
def method(index,title):
    c=copy.deepcopy(base['CHAPTERS'][index]);c['title']=title
    for key in ['deck','example','conclusion','task']:c[key]=c[key].replace('renovation','kitchen renovation').replace('Renovation','Kitchen renovation')
    c['paragraphs']=[p.replace('renovation','kitchen renovation') for p in c['paragraphs']]
    CHAPTERS.append(c)

method(0,'Define the kitchen project brief')
chapter('Record the existing kitchen','Document conditions before choosing replacements.',[
'Build a dated inventory of the room: cabinets, counters, flooring, fixtures, appliances, outlets, lighting and ventilation. Give each item a retain, replace, relocate or investigate status. An inventory helps a contractor understand the starting point and prevents a remembered detail from becoming an unexpected task later.',
'Photograph visible conditions from safe locations and keep the images with a simple sketch. Record door swings, windows, ceiling changes, access routes and adjoining finishes. Owner measurements are planning information; fabrication and installation dimensions must be confirmed by the responsible professionals before ordering.',
'Record evidence of water entry, damaged surfaces or past repairs as observations, not diagnoses. Ask the appropriate professional to establish the cause and proposed repair. An allowance can fund uncertainty, but it cannot establish whether a wall, floor or utility connection is suitable for the planned work.',
'Older finishes can affect demolition planning. EPA explains lead concerns associated with renovation work in older housing [2]. Discuss the building age and work scope with an appropriately qualified contractor before disturbing finishes; obtain project-specific advice on other suspect materials. This book provides no demolition or testing procedure.'
], 'The owner lists 14 existing components: eight retained, four replaced and two requiring investigation. The two unknown items remain open; they are not recorded as free work.', [('Retain','8 items'),('Replace','4 items'),('Investigate','2 items'),('Total inventory','14 items')],
'A count organizes decisions; it does not estimate repair severity or cost. Give each investigation an owner and deadline before affected purchases.',
['Room sketch / dated photo references','Retain / replace / relocate inventory','Observed defects / professional assessment needed','Building history / finish concerns / qualified reviewer','Open investigations / deadlines / dependent decisions'],
['Observations are distinguished from diagnoses.','Fabrication measurements have a professional owner.','No finish disturbance is authorized by this worksheet.'], 'Give every unresolved condition a named reviewer before inviting final bids.')

chapter('Choose layout changes deliberately','Retain, move or redesign with the full scope visible.',[
'Describe how the kitchen needs to work: cooking, storage, cleanup, seating and movement between spaces. Record who uses it and any accessibility needs that matter to the household. A functional brief gives a designer more useful information than choosing an island size before checking the room.',
'Compare a retained layout with a proposed change using complete installed scope. Moving a sink can affect supply, drainage, cabinetry, countertops, electrical work and surface restoration. Removing a wall may require professional structural review. Do not treat a sketch or this workbook as approval for changing framing or utilities.',
'Ask the designer and trades to confirm clearances, door and drawer operation, appliance openings and applicable requirements. This guide does not prescribe clearances or a universal layout rule. The useful deliverable is an approved, dated plan that the cabinet supplier and installing trades interpret the same way.',
'Record what each layout solves and what new work it creates. Keep optional features separate so they can be deferred without obscuring required repairs. Approve the final layout before long-lead orders, and issue a new version when a material dimension or appliance specification changes.'
], 'A fictional retained-layout proposal is $24,000. An otherwise comparable relocation option adds $1,800 plumbing, $900 electrical work and $600 restoration. Design and other differences are excluded here.', [('Retained-layout baseline','$24,000'),('Relocation scope additions','$3,300'),('Comparison for changed layout','$27,300'),('Layout premium','$3,300')],
'The premium is not a market benchmark. Confirm structural review, design, permits and other exclusions before treating either option as a complete budget.',
['Household needs / movement / accessibility priorities','Retained versus changed layout / drawing versions','Utility moves / structural questions / reviewers','Added installation and restoration scope / exclusions','Approved plan / approvers / order dependencies'],
['The full consequence of a move is recorded.','Trades use the same dated layout.','No unverified clearance is treated as an approved design.'], 'Ask for a scope-matched retained-layout alternative before approving a costly move.')

method(2,'Build the all-in kitchen budget')
method(3,'Write an installation-ready scope brief')

chapter('Specify cabinets as a complete package','Count the components behind the attractive door sample.',[
'Identify whether the work is cabinet replacement, refacing or a limited hardware and finish update. Those services have different removal and installation boundaries. For a replacement, record a cabinet schedule with each unit code, location, dimensions and proposed construction or performance specification.',
'Ask the supplier to identify fillers, end panels, toe kicks, moldings, finished backs, hinges, drawer systems and interior accessories. A cabinet-box price may omit several of these. Record delivery, assembly, installation and wall repair separately when they are not included in the same quote.',
'Coordinate openings with the exact appliances, sink, hood and countertop design before approving fabrication. Ask who checks the final dimensions, wall conditions and installation requirements. A supplier rendering is useful for discussion but should not replace an installer-approved schedule and written order confirmation.',
'Review lead times, damage reporting, returns and the handling of missing parts. Confirm the approval point after which a change affects price or delivery. Keep signed drawings, finish codes and accepted substitutions together so the delivered materials can be checked against the actual order.'
], 'A fictional cabinet quote includes $8,400 boxes and doors but excludes $650 panels, $250 trim, $300 delivery and $2,100 installation. These additions are documented separately.', [('Boxes and doors','$8,400'),('Panels + trim + delivery','$1,200'),('Installation','$2,100'),('Defined package total','$11,700')],
'Do not add installation if the cabinet price is already installed. This example excludes tax, demolition, wall repair and other trades.',
['Cabinet codes / sizes / construction / finish','Panels / fillers / trim / accessories / hardware','Appliance and sink coordination / approved drawings','Delivery / assembly / install / exclusions','Lead time / damage process / change cutoff / approver'],
['The quote covers more than cabinet boxes.','Openings match approved equipment specifications.','Order and installation responsibilities are clear.'], 'Request an itemized cabinet schedule and written inclusion list before paying the order deposit.')

chapter('Control hardware and storage upgrades','Small unit changes add up across the room.',[
'Count handles, knobs, hinges and selected storage accessories using the approved cabinet schedule. A drawer pull is not the same unit as a cabinet opening or a pair of doors. Use consistent item codes so the supplier, installer and owner can compare the same quantities.',
'Identify which hardware is already included with the cabinet order. Some upgrades replace an included item rather than adding a completely new one. Ask for the credit and upgrade amount in writing. A premium drawer insert should not conceal duplicated labor or a second charge for a component already in the base package.',
'Try the intended function and discuss placement with the designer or installer before drilling or fabrication. Record who confirms compatibility, finish and installation requirements. The workbook is an approval record, not a fitting template or instruction for modifying cabinetry.',
'For optional accessories, rank the practical benefit against the incremental installed cost. Choose the few functions the household will use regularly before adding every showroom feature. Record the final selection date so a late preference change does not create an avoidable replacement order.'
], 'Twenty-eight pulls are allowed at $8 each. The selected pull costs $12, with installation already included. A separately approved organizer adds $180 installed.', [('Original pull allowance','$224'),('Selected pulls: 28 × $12','$336'),('Pull overage','$112'),('Overage plus organizer','$292')],
'Only the $112 difference replaces the existing hardware allowance. The organizer is a separate optional addition. Tax and delivery are excluded here.',
['Hardware codes / quantities / unit definition','Included items / original allowances / credits','Chosen specification / compatibility / installer','Incremental material and labor change / exclusions','Selection approval / placement responsibility / deadline'],
['Included components have not been billed twice.','Upgrade credits are visible.','Drilling and placement follow the approved installation plan.'], 'Price hardware using the final item count, then approve the total difference in one record.')

chapter('Compare complete countertop scopes','Material area is only one part of the installed price.',[
'Identify the selected surface, thickness, finish, edge treatment and countertop configuration. Record the supplier and the person responsible for measuring, templating, fabrication and installation. An owner sketch can support an allowance, but the fabricator must confirm the actual dimensions before production.',
'Ask about sink and cooktop cutouts, seams, backsplashes, supports, delivery access and removal of the old surface. Some proposals price a minimum slab or fabricated package rather than only the visible counter area. Match those boundaries before comparing a rate per square foot.',
'Confirm when cabinets must be installed and accepted before templating. Coordinate the sink, faucet, appliance models and any required supports with the fabricator and relevant trades. A late appliance change can invalidate a previously approved cutout and create rework.',
'Separate disconnection and reconnection responsibility. If the fabricator excludes plumbing, the project still needs a named party and a price for it. Record protection of adjacent finishes and how damage or a fit issue is handled. This handbook does not specify structural supports or fabrication methods.'
], 'For fictional planning, 42 sq ft at an invented $80 installed rate gives $3,360. That rate expressly excludes a $250 cutout and $300 removal. Reconnection is separately priced elsewhere.', [('Area line: 42 × $80','$3,360'),('Excluded cutout','$250'),('Removal','$300'),('Defined countertop subtotal','$3,910')],
'Do not add cutouts or removal when they are already included in the installed rate. Confirm minimums, supports, tax and reconnect work separately.',
['Surface / finish / edge / fabricator specification','Area basis / slab minimum / confirmed template','Cutouts / seams / supports / included accessories','Removal / disconnect / reconnect / restoration owners','Cabinet readiness / equipment approval / dates'],
['The fabricator owns final dimensions.','The area rate has clear boundaries.','Every disconnected fixture has a reconnection owner.'], 'Send the exact sink and appliance specifications to the fabricator before final template approval.')

chapter('Plan sink, faucet and plumbing work','A fixture price does not describe the connection scope.',[
'List the sink, faucet and optional equipment with exact model references and the intended location. Identify what remains, what moves and who verifies compatibility. A sink purchase alone does not include removal, disposal, plumbing alterations, installation or reconnection unless the proposal says so.',
'Ask the plumbing professional to assess existing connections and any proposed relocation. Separate an observed problem from a diagnosis, and request a defined investigation where the condition is unclear. This book gives no instruction for modifying supply, drainage, gas or electrical systems.',
'Coordinate sink dimensions with the cabinet and countertop approvals. Record faucet mounting, accessory openings and any equipment connections on the common specification sheet. A disagreement between two suppliers is easier to resolve before fabrication than after the counter is cut.',
'Describe the finished scope and acceptance evidence with the installer: completed connections, checks appropriate to the work, cleanup and restoration. Confirm water interruption timing and how occupants will be informed. Keep warranty and service records with the final project documents.'
], 'A fictional plumbing allowance includes a $420 sink, $180 faucet, $600 defined labor and $100 disposal. The contractor has confirmed those lines do not overlap. No pipe relocation is included.', [('Sink and faucet','$600'),('Defined labor','$600'),('Disposal','$100'),('Plumbing package subtotal','$1,300')],
'The subtotal does not diagnose pipe condition or price relocation. Any additional work needs an assessed scope and written approval.',
['Exact fixture models / location / retained components','Existing-condition assessment / unresolved work','Cabinet / counter / mounting coordination','Included labor / disposal / interruptions / restoration','Completion checks / service records / warranty'],
['Product and installation costs are distinguished.','All trades share approved fixture specifications.','Additional repairs follow a defined change process.'], 'Obtain one coordinated fixture schedule before the cabinet and countertop orders are final.')

chapter('Choose appliances with installation in mind','Model numbers should drive the opening and connection record.',[
'Record each appliance by exact model, dimensions and manufacturer installation information. A label such as thirty-inch range is not enough to confirm the opening, ventilation arrangement or utility requirements. Ask the designer and installing professionals to verify the selected equipment before cabinet fabrication.',
'Compare delivered and installed scope. The product price may exclude delivery, hauling away the old unit, accessories, connection work or changes to adjacent cabinetry. Keep any owner-supplied appliance visible in the overall schedule and agree who is responsible for damage, storage and missing parts.',
'Use product energy information to compare similar capacities and functions. ENERGY STAR dishwasher guidance points buyers to the EnergyGuide label for model comparisons [3]. Actual operating cost depends on usage and utility rates; a catalog saving is not a guarantee for this household.',
'Coordinate delivery and inspection before the unit is needed. Record return windows, reported damage and the plan for a delayed model. A substitute needs a fresh compatibility check; do not assume a similar name means the same dimensions or connection requirements.'
], 'A fictional appliance costs $900, with $75 delivery, $50 haul-away and $140 installation accessories and labor. Taxes and any utility changes are outside this example.', [('Appliance','$900'),('Delivery and haul-away','$125'),('Defined install package','$140'),('Delivered and installed subtotal','$1,165')],
'If the seller includes delivery or installation, remove that duplicate line. Electrical, plumbing and cabinet changes need separate confirmed scope.',
['Model numbers / dimensions / installation documents','Delivered versus installed inclusions / exclusions','Energy labels / capacity / comparison assumptions','Utility and opening confirmation / responsible trades','Delivery / return / damage / substitution plan'],
['Models are approved before dependent fabrication.','Installation and accessory costs are visible.','Energy estimates are not promised household savings.'], 'Give the installer the model list and ask for written confirmation of the complete connection scope.')

chapter('Define electrical and lighting endpoints','Count complete tasks rather than a vague electrical allowance.',[
'List the appliances, lighting functions and outlet needs the household is planning. Give each proposed endpoint a location and purpose on the common layout. Distinguish replacing a visible fitting from altering circuits, opening walls or changing service equipment; those are different scopes.',
'Have the electrical professional assess capacity, existing conditions and applicable requirements. The owner worksheet does not size circuits, specify protection or authorize wiring work. Ask which investigations or permits are needed before the affected equipment orders become fixed.',
'For lighting, separate task, general and decorative decisions. Record fixture models, controls, supply responsibility and installation inclusions. A pendant price does not necessarily cover mounting, wiring, access or finish restoration. Confirm compatibility among selected fixtures and controls through the responsible installer.',
'Assign patching and painting after electrical work. Record temporary interruption dates and communication with occupants. Keep changes tied to endpoints and drawing versions so an upgrade request can be evaluated for its whole installed cost rather than only its fixture price.'
], 'A fictional quote defines six complete lighting endpoints at $150 each plus $280 fixture supply. The endpoint scope excludes painting, which is separately included in the finish contract.', [('Defined endpoints: 6 × $150','$900'),('Fixture supply','$280'),('Electrical subtotal','$1,180'),('Painting','In separate finish contract')],
'The rate is invented and does not specify a circuit or installation method. Confirm whether fixture supply is already in an installed quote before adding it.',
['Endpoint IDs / locations / purpose / drawing version','Professional assessment / permits / unknowns','Fixtures / controls / supply and install responsibility','Included access / patching / painting boundaries','Interruption plan / approvals / change record'],
['Capacity and requirements have a professional reviewer.','Fixtures and labor are not counted twice.','Finish restoration is assigned.'], 'Ask the electrician to mark inclusions and unanswered questions against the approved endpoint list.')

chapter('Coordinate cooking ventilation','Treat the hood and its route as one planning question.',[
'Record the cooking equipment and the proposed ventilation arrangement with the relevant designer and installation professionals. A hood product price does not establish a suitable complete installation. Ask who verifies the selected system, its route and the requirements for this building.',
'List the included equipment, duct or other specified components, external termination where applicable, electrical connection and surface restoration. Confirm which trade owns each interface with walls, ceilings or the roof. An unresolved route can change cabinets, finishes and schedule even when the hood itself is available.',
'Ask the professionals to identify applicable airflow, makeup-air, combustion or other design questions for the actual system. This book does not give a ventilation design, airflow rule or instruction for cutting a penetration. Record the verification and supporting documents rather than filling the gap with a guessed specification.',
'Keep installation and operating documentation with the appliance record. Ask how completion will be checked and what ongoing cleaning or service the installed equipment requires. A substitution should trigger a coordinated review before the replacement product is accepted.'
], 'A fictional complete ventilation scope separates $650 equipment, $480 defined route work, $170 connection work and $200 restoration. Design and any additional assessment are excluded.', [('Equipment','$650'),('Route work and connection','$650'),('Restoration','$200'),('Defined scope subtotal','$1,500')],
'The figures show scope addition only. They do not size equipment or establish a compliant route. Resolve excluded design questions before treating the scope as approved.',
['Cooking and hood models / proposed arrangement','Route / interfaces / accountable professional','Equipment / connection / termination / restoration scope','Design questions / required approvals / evidence','Completion check / manuals / maintenance / substitutions'],
['A professional owns system suitability.','The route and finish interfaces are assigned.','No airflow assumption is treated as a design.'], 'Request a coordinated ventilation scope before finalizing cabinets around the cooking area.')

chapter('Budget flooring and the surface underneath','Purchase quantity and installation scope answer different questions.',[
'Measure the floor zones used for planning and label the boundaries around cabinets and adjoining rooms. Ask the designer and installer how the selected flooring integrates with fixed cabinetry and appliance access. The installation sequence depends on the product and project; do not apply a universal rule from a photograph.',
'Separate measured area, layout allowance and packaged purchase quantity. Use the actual coverage printed for the selected product, then round to whole packages. Have the installer confirm the layout and quantity before ordering. A standard example allowance is not a product-specific requirement.',
'Budget removal, disposal, substrate assessment, preparation, installation, trim and transitions. Existing floor condition and height changes can affect doors and appliances. Record professional assessment of moisture or damage concerns before relying on a surface-only price.',
'Identify who moves appliances, disconnects and reconnects them, and protects the new surface during remaining work. Keep material instructions and warranty conditions with the order. Do not assume unused opened packages can be returned or that the final invoice will equal a rough area multiplied by a showroom rate.'
], 'A fictional 240 sq ft kitchen uses a selected 10% ordering allowance, giving 264 sq ft. Cartons cover 22 sq ft each and cost an invented $88. Installation is separately priced.', [('Area with allowance','264 sq ft'),('Cartons: 264 / 22','12 cartons'),('Material: 12 × $88','$1,056'),('Installation and preparation','Not included')],
'The allowance and carton coverage are example inputs. Removal, preparation, transitions, installation and tax must be checked separately.',
['Floor zones / boundaries / sequence confirmed by installer','Selected product / layout allowance / carton coverage','Measured and rounded quantities / approved takeoff','Removal / prep / transitions / height issues','Appliance movement / reconnect / protection / records'],
['Purchase area and installed area are distinguished.','Sequence matches selected product requirements.','Every appliance movement has an assigned owner.'], 'Have the installer confirm both the takeoff and preparation scope before material purchase.')

chapter('Finish the backsplash, walls and trim','The last visible surfaces still need preparation and boundaries.',[
'Define the exact backsplash extent and the wall and trim surfaces to be finished. Mark openings, edges, corners and transitions on a sketch. A tile unit price cannot establish whether substrate preparation, edge treatment, setting materials, sealing if specified or electrical fitting adjustments are included.',
'For painting, record the surface condition, preparation scope, products and agreed number of coats. Identify who repairs damage after cabinet, plumbing and electrical work. Keep gross wall area, deductions and any layout allowance traceable; the installer may price detailed areas differently from a simple area calculation.',
'Choose and approve samples with the people who need to fabricate or install the adjoining work. Finish decisions can affect lead times and detailing. Keep accepted samples, product codes and substitution approvals together rather than relying on a screen image viewed months earlier.',
'Confirm protection and final cleanup, including how unfinished punch-list items will be handled around a usable kitchen. Do not let a small remaining area become an unowned task between two trades. Budget the finished result, including touch-up responsibility, rather than only the products left in the boxes.'
], 'A fictional backsplash has 30 sq ft net area. A selected 10% allowance gives 33 sq ft. Packs cover 5 sq ft, so seven packs provide 35 sq ft. Each costs an invented $40.', [('Area plus allowance','33 sq ft'),('Packs: round up 33 / 5','7 packs'),('Purchased coverage','35 sq ft'),('Material: 7 × $40','$280')],
'This is material arithmetic only. Substrate work, installation, edges, adjustments and tax are not included. Ask the installer to confirm layout and order quantities.',
['Finish boundaries / openings / edge details','Surface preparation / patching / responsible trades','Approved samples / product codes / substitutions','Quantity basis / packages / included installation','Protection / touch-up / punch-list owner / cleanup'],
['Preparation is written into the scope.','Edge and transition responsibilities are clear.','Material subtotal is not presented as complete installed cost.'], 'Review a single finish schedule with all trades before the final material orders.')

chapter('Plan life without a working kitchen','Temporary arrangements belong in the real budget.',[
'List the functions that will be unavailable and the expected interruption dates: food storage, cooking, washing up and access through the room. Agree a practical temporary arrangement with the household and contractor. Use equipment only as its manufacturer permits, and keep temporary facilities away from the work area.',
'Record delivery and decision dependencies. Cabinets must be ready for the agreed countertop template stage; fixtures and appliances must be approved before dependent openings are finalized. Ask the responsible trades to confirm the actual sequence rather than imposing an assumed universal schedule.',
'Price storage, temporary food arrangements, protection and any accommodation the household expects to need. Separate ordinary household spending from the additional cost caused by the project. An extra meal allowance should represent the incremental amount, not every grocery purchase that would have happened anyway.',
'Keep a delay plan with communication contacts and the trigger for revising the temporary arrangement. A date in the initial proposal is not proof that every supplier is committed. Update the schedule when an approved change or delayed item affects a dependent task, and record the related owner-side cost.'
], 'A fictional household expects additional food costs of $18 per day for 21 days and $160 storage. These are incremental project costs; ordinary grocery spending is excluded.', [('Additional food: 21 × $18','$378'),('Storage','$160'),('Temporary arrangement subtotal','$538'),('Seven extra days at $18','$126 more')],
'Use your actual additional spending and dates. A delay changes this allowance; it does not authorize extra contractor charges without the agreed change procedure.',
['Unavailable functions / interruption dates / occupants','Temporary arrangement / manufacturer restrictions','Delivery and trade dependencies / confirmed dates','Incremental owner costs / ordinary spending excluded','Delay trigger / contacts / revised cost decision'],
['Temporary costs are inside the project budget.','Dependencies are confirmed by responsible trades.','A delay has a communication and spending response.'], 'Complete the temporary kitchen and delivery plan before demolition is scheduled.')

